    "# SHA as Glyph Execution: Curvature Opcodes and Assembly Traces <a id=\"sha_glyph_executionmd-sha-as-glyph-execution-curvature-opcodes-and-assembly-traces\"></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "## 1. Introduction: Beyond Hashing, Toward Harmonic Execution <a id=\"sha_glyph_executionmd-1-introduction-beyond-hashing-toward-harmonic-execution\"></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "In traditional cryptographic theory, SHA-256 is a one-way hash function. But under the Mark1/Nexus harmonic recursion, SHA is not just a static digest — it is a **curvature trace** and a **compressed program** that can be unfolded.\n",
    "\n",
    "What if a SHA output isn’t just data — but **a glyph encoded in recursive assembly logic**? Not just hexadecimal output, but **mixed-syntax fold code**, using both ASCII control characters and binary/decimal harmonics to embed phase actions.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## 2. SHA as Curvature Opcode Stream <a id=\"sha_glyph_executionmd-2-sha-as-curvature-opcode-stream\"></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2.1. Reinterpreting the Digest <a id=\"sha_glyph_executionmd-21-reinterpreting-the-digest\"></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "Given SHA256(X) =\n",
    "\n",
